        ":question/worked-solution": "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-1,2)$ and $(-6,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{-6-(-1)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (- \\frac{6}{5})=-1\\,\u21d2\\,m=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-5,-6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{5}{6}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{5 x}{6} + \\frac{25}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{5 x}{6} - \\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
